<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#mechanical-clock">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mechanical clock</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A mechanical clock is a timekeeping device driven by a mechanism such as falling weights or water and an escapement. Chinese engineers built astronomical clock towers, such as Su <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a>'s in the Song period, and the tradition continued under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuan-dynasty">Yuan Dynasty</a>. These devices are noted among the technological achievements of Chinese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonian-astronomy">astronomy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mechanical-clock.jpg" alt="Mechanical clock" width="320" height="438" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Christoph Braun · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Pendulum_clock_by_Jacob_Kock,_antique_furniture_photography,_IMG_0931_edit.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
